// mgeHost64 — alpha-to-coverage: the ONE definition of "which MSAA samples does this cutout cover".
//
// WHAT IT FIXES. MSAA runs the pixel shader ONCE per pixel and applies its result to every covered
// sample, so it antialiases GEOMETRIC edges only. A cutout edge — foliage, grates, fences — is made
// by `discard`, which is all-or-nothing per pixel, so it stays hard-aliased no matter how many
// samples the target has. Alpha-to-coverage turns the cutout's fractional alpha into a per-sample
// coverage mask, which is the only way an alpha test gets antialiased at all.
//
// PRIOR ART, AND WHY WE DIVERGE FROM IT. MGE's DX9 path already did this: `XE Common.fx:333`
//     float calc_coverage(float a, float ref, float rate) { return saturate(rate*(a-ref) + ref); }
// used by `XE Mod Statics.fx:93` (rate 2.0) and `XE Mod Grass.fx:116` (rate 4.0), feeding hardware
// A2C. `statics.frag.fsl`'s header has carried "real A2C is a live-path (MSAA) refinement" as a
// known gap ever since the port. We keep the mechanism and drop the constants
// ([[feedback_prior_art_constants_dont_transfer]]):
//
// **A FIXED rate cannot be stable with distance.** `rate*(a-ref)` is a slope in ALPHA, and the
// alpha of a mip-reduced leaf texture flattens toward its mean as the object recedes — so the same
// slope spans more and more of the surface, the cutout softens, and distant foliage dissolves into
// translucent mush. That is the classic alpha-test-under-mipmaps failure, and a rate knob can only
// choose WHICH distance it happens at.
//
// So the slope here is taken from the SCREEN, not from a constant: dividing by `fwidth(alpha)`
// makes the transition exactly one pixel wide at every distance and every mip level, which is
// scale-invariant by construction. Distant foliage keeps its shape instead of fading out; near
// foliage gets a soft edge instead of a staircase.
//
// **WHY THERE IS NO WIDTH KNOB.** One pixel is not a taste setting, it is the width at which the
// coverage ramp matches the sampling rate — wider blurs the cutout, narrower re-aliases it. The
// tunable thing (which passes participate, and whether A2C runs at all) is the on/off toggle.
//
// ⚠ THE PREPASS/COLOUR PARITY IS THE WHOLE RISK. The colour PSOs test `CMP_EQUAL` against the
// Z-prepass, so the prepass and the colour pass must agree on coverage BIT FOR BIT — a pixel whose
// two passes disagree fails EQUAL and vanishes. That is why this is a shared header called from
// both sides rather than two copies of the same three lines: they cannot drift. The existing code
// already depended on this (`depthonly.frag`: "Strict '<' must MATCH the colour pass exactly"); A2C
// only raises the stakes.
//
// ⚠ `fwidth` NEEDS UNIFORM CONTROL FLOW. Derivatives come from the 2x2 quad, so every caller must
// reach this function with the whole quad intact — i.e. call it BEFORE any discard, never inside a
// branch that can differ across a quad. All current callers sample albedo and come straight here.
#pragma once

// gFrameData.alphaShadowParams.w carries the MSAA sample count when A2C is on and 0 when it is off
// — ONE lane doing both jobs, because it is the last spare float4 component in a FrameData that is
// exactly 512 B against a 512 B CBV (opaque.srt.h:145). The count has to be a uniform: these frags
// are shared by every pass, so it cannot be a per-variant #define the way resolve.frag's is.
#define A2C_SAMPLES (gFrameData.alphaShadowParams.w)

// Coverage mask for a cutout pixel. Returns 0 (kill the pixel) or a mask of covered samples.
//
// A2C OFF, or 1x, or a non-alpha-tested draw (alphaRef == 0) => the classic binary answer, so this
// is a drop-in for `a < ref ? discard : keep` and the A/B is exact.
uint a2cCoverageMask(float alpha, float alphaRef)
{
    // Unconditionally, before any branch: see the uniform-control-flow note above.
    float aw = fwidth(alpha);

    float samples = A2C_SAMPLES;
    if (samples < 1.5f || alphaRef <= 0.0f)
    {
        return (alpha < alphaRef) ? 0u : ~0u;
    }

    // One-pixel-wide ramp centred on the reference: 0.5 at a == ref, so the 50% coverage contour
    // sits exactly where the binary test used to flip. That keeps the silhouette in the same place
    // as the A/B and confines the change to the edge pixels.
    float cov = saturate((alpha - alphaRef) / max(aw, 1.0e-5f) + 0.5f);

    // Fraction -> mask. Ordered and DETERMINISTIC (no per-pixel dither): a stochastic mask would
    // give a nominally better distribution and would crawl under any camera motion, and "stable" is
    // the requirement here. Bits are taken in order; the D3D standard 4x/8x sample positions are
    // spread across the pixel, so any prefix of them is a reasonable spatial subset.
    uint n = (uint)(cov * samples + 0.5f);
    if (n == 0u)                { return 0u; }
    if (n >= (uint)(samples))   { return ~0u; }
    return (1u << n) - 1u;
}

// Same mask, with an extra COVERAGE scale in [0,1] — the G1 grass distance fade.
//
// WHY THE FADE MULTIPLIES THE COVERAGE AND NOT THE ALPHA. Scaling alpha before the test moves the
// contour to alpha == ref/scale, which erodes the blade inward from wherever its alpha ramp happens
// to be. Grass alpha is very nearly binary (a leaf mask is 0 or 255 over most of its area), so that
// erodes almost nothing and then drops the whole blade at once when scale falls below ref — MGE's
// hard pop, moved a few metres. Scaling the COVERAGE instead thins the sample MASK: the blade keeps
// its shape and loses samples, which under MSAA reads as it becoming sparse and then gone. That is
// what a receding grass field is supposed to do.
//
// ⚠ THE ORIGINAL ABOVE IS LEFT BYTE-IDENTICAL ON PURPOSE, rather than being expressed as a call to
// this one with scale = 1. Its callers (statics.frag, depthonly.frag, the shadow frags) are on the
// "grass off must be bit-identical to today" check, which is verified by diffing their DXIL — and a
// refactor that is only *provably* equivalent after DXC's constant folding turns a one-look proof
// into an argument. Two short bodies is the cheaper price.
uint a2cCoverageMaskFaded(float alpha, float alphaRef, float coverScale)
{
    float aw = fwidth(alpha);   // unconditionally, before any branch (see above)

    float samples = A2C_SAMPLES;
    if (samples < 1.5f || alphaRef <= 0.0f)
    {
        // No MSAA to spend on the fade: it degrades to the binary test plus a hard cut where the
        // scale reaches zero. Still monotone, still no crawl — just not antialiased.
        return (alpha < alphaRef || coverScale <= 0.0f) ? 0u : ~0u;
    }

    float cov = saturate((alpha - alphaRef) / max(aw, 1.0e-5f) + 0.5f) * saturate(coverScale);

    uint n = (uint)(cov * samples + 0.5f);
    if (n == 0u)                { return 0u; }
    if (n >= (uint)(samples))   { return ~0u; }
    return (1u << n) - 1u;
}
